  "body": "Found while fixing #1217 (PR #1228). PR #1228 limits the solid law's full chord-dip credit to facet edges whose ends lie on a DECLARED hole rim; all other edges get at most 0.01 mm.\n\n**Remaining gap (a gouge that can pass):** if a coarse polygonal hole is declared or recognized at its own CORNER circle (the circumscribed radius), every flat's ends lie on that rim, so the flats keep the full chord-dip credit. A drill, bore or thread of that diameter then cuts into the flats by up to the chord dip and is not convicted. Only the hole recognizer (or the model's own surface type) can tell a real circle from a polygon here.\n\n**Done means:** a hole recognized from a polygonal wall (flats, not a tessellated circle, e.g. too few facets for the source tolerance, or the source surface type isn't cylindrical) gets no rim credit, or is declared at its inscribed radius; a drill of the corner-circle diameter in an octagon convicts; matched drills in tessellated round holes (the drill_plate / tap_plate goldens, the 16-gon r 2 case in `operations`) stay clean.\n",
